    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on a loft addition for a residential build out in Oregon. i'm using Douglas Fir for the main support beams. i need to calculate the max span for a 6x12 beam to support a live load of 40psf plus the dead load of the floor system. can u help me figure out the deflection limits i should be looking at so i don't end up with a bouncy floor? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For Douglas Fir at 6x12 pitch, considering a live load plus dead load, you'd want to check the span tables that take into account both the load and deflection criteria specified in the building code. Typically, for a residential building in Oregon, deflection limits should not exceed span/360 for the allowable deflection under the live load only, and span/240 for the working load. So, for your 6x12 Douglas Fir beam, checking with a span table or calculating using I-beam design formulas considering a deflection limit of span/360 should ensure a comfortable floor that won't feel bouncy. Remember to account for the bearing spacing and support conditions as well, as these will also impact your beam deflection.",
